Equipment and/or Software Needs / Costs:
Equipment, Software, and Course Development Needs (Estimated Costs)
The UAS / Drone Technology Program requires specialized equipment, software=
, and course development resources to support instruction aligned with indu=
stry standards. The following cost estimates are preliminary and are intend=
ed to provide a reasonable projection for program startup and phased expans=
ion. Actual costs may vary based on vendor pricing, educational discounts, =
and partnership opportunities.

A Note on NDAA Compliance and Fleet Diversification
The National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA) and the American Security Dro=
ne Act (ASDA), embedded in the FY2024 NDAA and fully enforced as of Decembe=
r 22, 2025, prohibit the use of federal funds to purchase or operate drones=
 manufactured by companies from designated covered foreign countries, prima=
rily China. This restriction applies to any program procuring drones using =
federal funds awarded through contracts, grants, or cooperative agreements=
=97including Perkins (Strengthening Career and Technical Education for the =
21st Century Act) funds. As a practical matter, this means that Perkins-fun=
ded drone purchases must be sourced from NDAA-compliant platforms, and DJI =
and Autel drones are no longer eligible for procurement under that funding =
stream.
It is important to note that the Strong Workforce Program (SWP) is funded t=
hrough the California state budget and is not a federal grant program. As s=
uch, the ASDA=92s procurement restrictions do not apply as a direct legal m=
andate to SWP expenditures. However, the program is recommending NDAA-compl=
iant platforms for all new equipment purchases regardless of funding source=
, for the following strategic reasons: (1) the Perkins funding stream is su=
bject to the restriction and must remain compliant; (2) industry partners i=
n the program=92s primary employment sectors=97utilities, public safety, an=
d government agencies=97have already mandated or are actively transitioning=
 to NDAA-compliant fleets, and training students on non-compliant platforms=
 would disadvantage graduates in these markets; and (3) the regulatory envi=
ronment is continuing to tighten, and purchasing compliant equipment now pr=
otects the program=92s long-term investment.
Exposing students to the NDAA-compliant ecosystem=97including platforms val=
idated through the Department of Defense=92s Blue UAS Cleared List=97prepar=
es graduates for the direction the broader commercial and public-sector dro=
ne industry is moving. PG&E, Cal OES, and the local government partners who=
 have endorsed this program are among the agencies actively transitioning a=
way from Chinese-manufactured platforms. Graduates trained on compliant sys=
tems will be ready to work in these environments without additional retrain=
ing.
In response to this landscape, the program will utilize a diversified fleet=
 of purpose-specific platforms. Rather than 6=968 identical entry-level dro=
nes, the program will acquire a smaller number of training platforms supple=
mented by mission-specific enterprise drones aligned to each instructional =
track. This approach reduces redundancy, increases student exposure to the =
types of platforms they will encounter in industry, and positions the progr=
am for compliance across all current and anticipated future funding require=
ments.

Year 1 =96 Core Program Launch (Mapping, Inspection Foundations)
Initial investments will support AERO 100, AERO 110 (Mapping), and AERO 120=
 (Inspection).
UAS Platforms =96 Diversified Training Fleet

  *   3=964 NDAA-compliant entry-level / general flight training UAS (e.g.,=
 Parrot ANAFI USA or equivalent Blue UAS-cleared platform) =96 used for fou=
ndational flight skills in AERO 100, NIST competency testing, and basic mis=
sion practice. Estimated unit cost: $5,000=96$7,000 each.
  *   DJI Mavic 3E (Enterprise mapping platform) =96 supports AERO 110 (Dro=
ne Mapping and Photogrammetry). Already owned by the College, purchased thr=
ough prior SWP funding in anticipation of program launch. The DJI Mavic 3E =
features a 4/3 CMOS mechanical shutter sensor and supports RTK workflows fo=
r professional orthomosaic and photogrammetry production. As SWP is a Calif=
ornia state-funded program (not a federal grant), this prior purchase is no=
t subject to ASDA/NDAA federal procurement restrictions. This platform will=
 serve as the primary mapping instruction tool in Year 1. Estimated cost to=
 program: $0 (College-owned).
  *   DJI Mavic 3T (Thermal inspection platform) =96 supports AERO 120 (The=
rmal Imaging and Applied Inspection). Already owned by the College, purchas=
ed through prior SWP funding in anticipation of program launch. The DJI Mav=
ic 3T features a wide-angle camera, a 640=D7512 radiometric thermal sensor,=
 and a 56=D7 hybrid zoom camera, making it well-suited for utility inspecti=
on, solar panel assessment, and building envelope analysis instruction. As =
with the 3E, this platform was acquired with state SWP funds and is not sub=
ject to federal NDAA procurement restrictions. Estimated cost to program: $=
0 (College-owned).

UAS Platforms (Year 1) Estimated Cost: $15,000 =96 $28,000 (mapping and the=
rmal platforms provided by existing College inventory; new spend limited to=
 general training UAS)
Batteries, Charging Stations, and Field Kits Estimated Cost: $10,000 =96 $1=
5,000
Mapping and Photogrammetry Software Estimated Cost: $5,000 =96 $10,000
Lab Equipment and Storage Estimated Cost: $10,000 =96 $20,000

Course Development (Year 1)
Course development is required to design a curriculum aligned with FAA stan=
dards and industry workflows, including development of Course Outlines of R=
ecord (all courses), lab exercises, and assessments.
Estimated Cost: $15,000 =96 $25,000

Year 1 Total Estimated Cost: $40,000 =96 $73,000 (reflects $0 cost for Coll=
ege-owned DJI Mavic 3E and 3T)

Year 2 =96 Program Expansion (Videography + Agriculture Monitoring + Spray =
Operations)
Equipment and Software

  *   1=962 NDAA-compliant multispectral sensor drones (e.g., senseFly eBee=
 X with Parrot Sequoia+ multispectral payload, or AgEagle eBee TAC) =96 sup=
ports AERO 130 (Multispectral Imaging and Crop Monitoring). Fixed-wing or h=
ybrid VTOL platforms provide extended coverage area ideal for precision agr=
iculture applications. Estimated unit cost: $15,000=96$25,000 each.
  *   1 NDAA-compliant agricultural spray drone system (e.g., Hylio AG-272 =
or equivalent U.S.-assembled spray platform) =96 supports AERO 140 (Agricul=
tural Spray Operations). Must support FAA Part 137 operations and Californi=
a QAC/QAL chemical application requirements. Estimated cost: $20,000=96$35,=
000.
  *   Specialized training consumables: spray tank and nozzle calibration k=
its, chemical handling safety equipment, PPE, and field containment supplie=
s for spray operations training.
  *   Additional batteries, accessories, and media production support equip=
ment for the Videography track.
  *   Media and GIS software integration (additional licenses, expanded clo=
ud processing capacity).

Estimated Cost: $45,000 =96 $80,000

Course Development (Year 2)
Course development is required to design a curriculum aligned with FAA stan=
dards and industry workflows, including the development of exercises and as=
sessments.
Estimated Cost: $10,000 =96 $20,000

Year 3 =96 Advanced Applications (Public Safety)
Equipment

  *   1 dedicated public safety / DFR platform (e.g., Skydio X10 or BRINC L=
EMUR 2) =96 supports the UAS Public Safety track (AERO 121=96123). The Skyd=
io X10 is a Blue UAS-cleared, American-made platform with AI-powered obstac=
le avoidance, integrated thermal imaging, and 5G connectivity, widely adopt=
ed by law enforcement and fire departments. Estimated cost: $10,000=96$20,0=
00.
  *   Specialized training equipment for public safety operations: portable=
 lighting, night operations accessories, and simulated incident command fie=
ld kit.

Estimated Cost: $15,000 =96 $30,000

Course Development (Year 3)
Course development is required to design a curriculum aligned with FAA stan=
dards and industry workflows, including the development of exercises and as=
sessments.
Estimated Cost: $5,000 =96 $10,000

Total Estimated Investment (3-Year Implementation)

  *   Equipment & Software: $100,000 =96 $178,000 (net of $0 cost for Colle=
ge-owned DJI Mavic 3E and 3T)
  *   Course Development (Year 1): $15,000 =96 $25,000
  *   Course Development (Years 2 & 3): $15,000 =96 $30,000

Total Estimated Investment: $130,000 =96 $233,000

Narrative Justification
All costs provided are estimates intended to support program planning and d=
evelopment. The phased approach to equipment acquisition reflects a strateg=
y of aligning resources with program growth, instructional readiness, and i=
ndustry engagement. This approach allows the college to prioritize core ins=
tructional needs at launch while scaling investment as additional courses a=
nd specialization areas are introduced.
A significant cost offset in Year 1 is the College=92s existing inventory o=
f a DJI Mavic 3E and DJI Mavic 3T, both purchased through prior SWP funding=
 in anticipation of program launch. These platforms will serve as the prima=
ry mapping and thermal inspection instruction tools in Year 1, eliminating =
the need to purchase enterprise-grade equivalents in those categories. Beca=
use they were acquired with California state SWP funds rather than federal =
grant funds, this prior procurement is not subject to ASDA/NDAA restriction=
s. New equipment purchases under this proposal, particularly those funded t=
hrough Perkins, will be sourced from NDAA-compliant platforms. The program =
remains well-positioned to leverage Strong Workforce Program (SWP) funding,=
 Perkins, and regional workforce initiatives to offset remaining costs and =
support program expansion.


